<section xid="8" props="page-margin-footer:0.5in; page-margin-header:0.5in">
<p style="Normal" xid="9"><c>The United States began </c><c props="font-weight:bold">deploying naval assets to the Caribbean in August 2025</c><c> as part of Operation Southern Spear, with the stated goal of combating drug cartels. By December, the United States Southern Command reported approximately 15,000 troops were in the Caribbean region. This deployment included a substantial number of military assets, such as the USS Gerald R. Ford aircraft carrier, F-35 fighter jets, and various warships and surveillance aircraft. The U.S. also upgraded a long-abandoned Cold War naval base in the Caribbean, suggesting preparations for sustained operations.</c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="1"><c></c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="2"><c>The military buildup was accompanied by a campaign of strikes against alleged drug smuggling boats. Since early September 2025, the U.S. military carried out more than 30 such strikes, killing over 110 people, and by October 24, a tenth vessel was attacked. In late December 2025,</c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="5"><c>the </c><c props="font-weight:normal">CIA conducted a drone strike on a port facility in Venezuela</c><c> which was reportedly disclosed by President Trump. This event marked the first known U.S. land strike inside the country. Multiple news outlets, citing sources familiar with the matter, reported that the strike targeted a remote dock on the Venezuelan coast that U.S. officials believed was used by the Venezuelan transnational gang Tren de Aragua to store narcotics and prepare them for shipment. According to reports, no one was on the dock at the time of the strike, and no casualties occurred.</c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="6"><c></c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="7"><c></c><c>President Donald Trump indicated that ground attacks in Venezuela would begin "pretty soon" and later announced that the U.S. had carried out air operations targeting military facilities in Venezuela, including near the capital Caracas, on January 3, 2026. This operation culminated in the capture of Venezuelan President Nicolás Maduro and his wife, Cilia Flores, who were subsequently taken to New York to face charges.</c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="3"><c></c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="4"><c>The United States </c><c props="font-weight:bold">began seizing oil tankers off the coast of Venezuela in December 2025</c><c> as part of an escalated pressure campaign against the government of Nicolás Maduro. The first seizure, of a tanker named the Skipper, occurred on December 10, 2025. Following this, other tankers like the MT Centuries were also seized, and the US pursued additional vessels, including the Bella 1. These actions were taken under the Trump administration, which had previously announced a "total and complete blockade" of sanctioned oil tankers entering and leaving Venezuela.</c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="10"><c>The US government, including entities like the FBI, Homeland Security Investigations, and the Coast Guard, executed seizure warrants for these tankers. The US alleges that these vessels were transporting sanctioned oil from Venezuela and Iran, operating as part of an illicit oil shipping network supporting foreign terrorist organizations, or were otherwise linked to Venezuela's government and its alleged criminal activities, including drug trafficking. Venezuela's government, in turn, denounced these seizures as acts of piracy and theft.</c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="27"><c></c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="28"><c>Venezuelan President Nicolás Maduro </c><c props="font-weight:bold">attempted to de-escalate tensions with President Donald Trump</c><c> by proposing various measures, including offering a dominant stake in Venezuela’s oil and other mineral wealth to American companies and reversing oil exports from China to the United States. He also offered to slash energy and mining contracts with Chinese, Iranian, and Russian firms. Other proposals included a plan for Maduro to step down in three years, handing over power to his vice president, Delcy Rodriguez, to complete his six-year term ending in January 2031. He also sent a letter to Trump advocating for dialogue and rejecting drug trafficking allegations. Maduro made multiple offers of mediation and resources to ease U.S. tensions, and was willing to negotiate an agreement with the United States to combat drug trafficking.</c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="31"><c>President Trump, however, </c><c props="font-weight:bold">rejected these proposals from Maduro</c><c>. The White House questioned the legitimacy of Maduro’s rule and accused him of overseeing a narco-terrorist state, leading to the rejection of the proposal for Maduro to step down. Trump also stated that Maduro was offering “everything” because he did not want to engage in conflict with the United States.</c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="32"><c></c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="33"><c></c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="29"><c></c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="30"><c></c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="13"><c></c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="14"><c>Venezuelan opposition leader </c><c props="font-weight:bold">Maria Corina Machado was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize for 2025</c><c> by the Norwegian Nobel Committee. The committee cited her tireless work promoting democratic rights for the people of Venezuela and her struggle to achieve a just and peaceful transition from dictatorship to democracy. She was recognized for uniting the opposition and steadfastly resisting the militarization of Venezuelan society, embodying hope for a future where fundamental rights are protected and voices are heard. Her daughter, Ana Corina Sosa, accepted the award on her behalf in Oslo and delivered a lecture written by her mother. The award came amidst high tensions between the Maduro government and the United States. Machado had been in hiding for several months due to fears of arrest, making her journey to collect the award a perilous one. Ultimately, she arrived safely in Oslo to collect her Nobel Peace Prize just before midnight on December 10, 2025, after escaping from Venezuela, skirting military checkpoints and endured rough seas with U.S. support. </c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="21"><c>She </c><c props="font-weight:bold">vowed to privatize Venezuela's oil industry</c><c> and other national assets. She has openly stated that American companies will have significant investment opportunities and "make a lot of money" in Venezuela if her plans are implemented. This initiative is part of a broader "massive privatization program" that she claims could be a $1.7 trillion opportunity involving oil, natural gas, infrastructure, gold, and other minerals. Machado explicitly made these statements in an interview with Donald Trump Jr. in February, emphasizing Venezuela's vast oil reserves as a key attraction for international investment. She has also promoted her privatization plans to groups of U.S. corporate executives in Miami, Florida.</c></p>
<p style="Normal" xid="25"><c></c></p>
